# ── Sylvan Librarian on Cloudflare ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Deploy model: create a Worker in the Cloudflare dashboard → connect this git
# repo (Workers Builds) → deploy. That's it — no secrets, no payment method,
# no build variables. The KV namespace holding the card index is provisioned
# on first deploy, and the deploy itself builds and publishes the index, so a
# green build means a working site. A nightly cron refreshes it.
#
# Everything below is OPTIONAL. Set in the Cloudflare dashboard under
# Worker → Settings → Variables (or `wrangler secret put <NAME>`). This file
# is documentation only — nothing reads .env at runtime.

# ── Optional secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Trusted-caller bypass for the built-in per-IP rate limit. Unset (the
# default) = no bypass exists. Set it, and requests carrying a matching
# `X-API-Key` header skip the limiter — for your own server-to-server
# callers, whose traffic funnels through shared egress IPs and must not be
# counted per-IP. Comma-separated: give each caller its own key so one can
# be revoked without rotating the rest (whitespace around entries is
# ignored; keys therefore must not contain commas — the generator below
# cannot produce one). Generate each: openssl rand -hex 32
TRUSTED_API_KEYS=

# ── Optional plain variables ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Per-IP rate limiting on the engine routes (token bucket in a per-IP
# Durable Object — globally exact). OFF unless RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true is
# set. Both are runtime vars: change them in the dashboard, no redeploy.
# RATE_LIMIT_PER_10S defaults to 25 when unset — about 5 requests/second in
# practice, since async enforcement lets roughly 2x the configured number
# through. That is half of what Scryfall asks of its own API consumers, and
# far above any human: only cache MISSES ever reach the limiter.
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
RATE_LIMIT_PER_10S=25

# Cap on per-region engine fan-out (engine-<region>-1..N). A shard holds the
# store in memory and caches it decompressed in its own DO SQLite, so an extra
# shard costs duration plus ~88MB of the free plan's 5GB — released again on the
# nightly publish once the shard is no longer in the fan-out. Defaults to 8,
# which is a blast radius rather than a scaling limit: expansion is driven by
# measured signals and signals can be wrong. A new shard takes no traffic until
# it has warmed, so opening one never makes a user wait. Set 0 for genuinely
# unbounded horizontal scaling.
SHARDS_MAX=

# The reverse proxies, if any, allowed to rename this deployment's public
# origin. Unset (the default, and right for the workers.dev deployment and for
# anything fronted directly by Cloudflare) means the `X-Proxy-Host` and
# `x-forwarded-proto` headers are IGNORED and each request's own URL decides.
#
# Upstream trusts X-Proxy-Host unconditionally, and can: it is a private VPS
# whose only caller is its own proxy. This is a public Worker with an edge
# cache, and those headers build every absolute URL a card object carries
# (`uri`, `set_uri`, `rulings_uri`, `prints_search_uri`) plus a search's
# `next_page` — none of which are part of the cache key. Trusting them
# unconditionally let ONE request seed a 16-hour cache entry, served to
# everyone, whose every link pointed at the attacker's host.
#
# Set this only if a proxy you run really does rewrite the host, listing the
# public name it presents. Comma-separated, matched in full and exactly
# (include the port if the proxy sends one; no suffix matching, so a rule for
# cards.example.com never admits evil-cards.example.com). A value not on the
# list is ignored rather than rejected — the request is answered under this
# deployment's own name.
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOSTS=

# Point the import at a Scryfall bulk-data mirror (tests use a local fake).
SCRYFALL_BULK_URL=
